%I A399583 #26 Sep 12 2026 18:38:38
%S A399583 5,6,9,12,18,24,31,36,48,50,61,66,76,80,82,93,97,107,110,120,132,155,
%T A399583 158,167,185,200,209,214,221,226,270,273,275,283,290,329,332,337,340,
%U A399583 348,365,386,400,402,405,410,440,446,454,455,487,489,502,510,517,534
%N A399583 a(n) = floor((4*p+3)*Product_{q prime, q<=p}(1-1/q)) with p = A001359(n).
%C A399583 Integer floors of a sieve estimate for the number of primes between squares of twin primes.
%C A399583 The factor 4*p+3 is exactly the number of integers strictly between p^2 and (p+2)^2, since (p+2)^2 - p^2 - 1 = 4*p+3.
%C A399583 Let 2=q_1<q_2<...<q_k=p be the primes up to p, with q_0=1. S(p) = (4*p+3)*Product_{i=1..k}((q_i-1)/q_i) = ((4*p+3)/p)*Product_{i=1..k}((q_i-1)/q_{i-1}).
%C A399583 Since q_i and q_{i-1} are consecutive primes, q_i - 1 >= q_{i-1}, and every factor (q_i-1)/q_{i-1} in the product is at least 1. Because (4p+3)/p > 4, it follows that S(p)>4. Therefore every sequence term satisfies a(n) >= 4.
%C A399583 S(p) is a sieve-based estimate of the number of primes strictly between p^2 and (p+2)^2. It is not claimed to be always an upper or lower bound for the actual prime count. For example, S(59)=31.9735... for the twin-prime pair (59,61), while there are 32 primes strictly between 59^2 and 61^2.
%H A399583 Michael R. Page, <a href="https://doi.org/10.13140/RG.2.2.15519.94886">Probabilistic Estimates of the Number of Primes Between Squares of Successive Primes (Including Twin Primes)</a>.
%e A399583 The third lesser twin prime is p = 11, corresponding to the twin-prime pair (11,13). There are 13^2 - 11^2 - 1 = 47 integers strictly between the two squares. Applying the sieve factors for the primes 2, 3, 5, 7, and 11 gives S(11) = 47*(1/2)*(2/3)*(4/5)*(6/7)*(10/11) = 9.766233... . Therefore a(3) = floor(S(11)) = 9.
%o A399583 (SageMath)
%o A399583 def a(n):
%o A399583     p = 3
%o A399583     for k in range(1,n):
%o A399583         p = next_prime(p)
%o A399583         while not is_prime(p+2):
%o A399583             p = next_prime(p)
%o A399583     s = 4*p+3
%o A399583     for q in prime_range(p+1):
%o A399583         s *= (q-1)/q
%o A399583     return floor(s)
%o A399583 [a(n) for n in range(1,57)]
%Y A399583 Cf. A001359 (lesser of twin primes), A006512 (greater of twin primes), A137859 (number of primes between the n-th squared twin prime pairs).
%K A399583 nonn,new
%O A399583 1,1
